    "who_what": [
      {
        "text": "The repository describes agent-memory-mcp as a Go MCP server and CLI for persistent agent memory and project-document search; the implementation has separate memory, RAG, file-access and stewardship components.",
        "sources": [
          "repo",
          "readme",
          "architecture",
          "contributing"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "architecture": [
      {
        "text": "Memory types are episodic, semantic, procedural and working. SQLite rows carry content, title, tags, context, importance, metadata, embedding/model identity, timestamps, access counters and temporal/supersession fields; the store also maintains an in-process cache.",
        "sources": [
          "memory",
          "sql",
          "read"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The architecture guide separates memory storage, a SQLite-backed RAG document index, protocol tools, allowlisted file access and stewardship. Embedding model identity is retained; changing models requires reindexing and re-embedding.",
        "sources": [
          "architecture",
          "installation"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "behavior": [
      {
        "text": "Store validates input, rejects provider tokens and redacts credential-shaped content, computes optional embeddings before the write lock, inserts and reads back the SQLite row, updates cache and may asynchronously extract triples. Update also embeds outside the write lock and may continue without a vector if embedding fails.",
        "sources": [
          "write",
          "sql"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Revision code records prior content and title transactionally. Documentation exposes mark_outdated with temporal validity and supersession; RecallAsOf filters by validity and can return historical revision text. These are source observations, not measured correction accuracy.",
        "sources": [
          "revisions",
          "temporal",
          "tools"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The official tool guide exposes delete_memory by ID. The bounded review did not establish physical deletion across SQLite revisions, vector indexes, cache and derived state, so deletion completeness is unknown.",
        "sources": [
          "tools",
          "write",
          "sql"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The tool guide documents semantic/text recall with optional filters and trust-aware ranking, plus separate hybrid document search. Context is a supported filter; the code has context snapshots and a pre-cache-load database query. These interfaces do not establish recall quality or calibrated abstention.",
        "sources": [
          "tools",
          "read"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The local install guide describes a per-project state directory. Shared HTTP mode documents bearer-token and host/origin checks; repository file tools are constrained to MCP_ROOT and allowlisted paths. Context filtering is not itself an authenticated tenant boundary; deployment isolation was not exercised.",
        "sources": [
          "installation",
          "security",
          "memory"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Trust metadata derives layer, source type, confidence, owner and freshness from memory metadata, lifecycle, tags and timestamps. Stewardship docs describe verification and source drift checks; these signals do not prove a source currently supports a memory.",
        "sources": [
          "trust",
          "stewardship",
          "tools"
        ]
      }
    ],
